    <string name="rules_no_blinds">No blinds</string>
    <string name="rules_manual_play">The table does not know which game you are playing: it only counts chips. It works for any game with betting — canari, truc, mus, monte — and for every game without a mode of its own.</string>
    <string name="rules_manual_count">Everyone bets into the pot from their own phone. The host gives the pot to whoever won it, whole or in parts, and can open more pots. A pot nobody takes stays on the table for the next hand.</string>
    <string name="rules_manual_people">The rules, the turns and who wins each hand. The app only writes down what the host says, and anything can be undone from the log.</string>
    <string name="rules_seven_half_play">Played with a 40-card Spanish deck. Number cards are worth their number and the face cards (sota, caballo, rey) half a point. Each player draws to get as close to seven and a half as possible without going over, playing alone against the bank.</string>
    <string name="rules_seven_half_count">Whoever holds the bank banks it with their own chips, and the others stake against it before the deal. When the hand is over, the bank (or the host) settles each stake from the table: wins (the bank pays the same again), loses (the stake goes to the bank), push (the stake comes back) or seven and a half (the bank pays what this table says). If the bank busts, Pay everyone settles everybody left at once. If the bank cannot pay, it has to add chips. Anybody may take the bank while nobody holds it.</string>
    <string name="rules_seven_half_people">Who holds the bank and when it passes on, which hand counts as a seven and a half, and who takes a tie: in many places the bank does, and then the button to press is Loses. The bank is free to draw or stand, going by what it sees of the others.</string>
    <string name="rules_blackjack_play">Each player tries to get as close to 21 as possible without going over. Face cards are worth 10, an ace 1 or 11, the rest their number. An ace and a ten-card as the first two cards is a blackjack. Everyone plays against the house.</string>
    <string name="rules_blackjack_count">The bank is the house: it has chips of its own, takes no seat and does not bet; whoever deals runs it. Players stake before the deal and can double or split in one tap; each split hand is settled on its own. When the hand is over, the dealer (or the host) settles each hand from the table: wins, loses, push, blackjack (the house pays what this table says) or surrenders (half the stake back).</string>
    <string name="rules_blackjack_people">The house decides nothing: it draws on 16 or less and stands on 17 or more. Who won each hand, and the other house rules, are yours to settle.</string>
    <string name="rules_poker_play">Texas hold\'em: each player gets two cards and five are shared, dealt in three rounds. There is a round of betting before and after each, and the best five-card hand wins.</string>
    <string name="rules_poker_count">New hand moves the dealer and posts the blinds. The big button says exactly what a call costs. Between rounds, the host closes the round. If somebody goes all-in with fewer chips, Split the pot separates the pots each player can win, and the host awards them one by one. Once the last of the pot is awarded, the next hand deals itself. If everybody but one folds, the pot is theirs without anybody doing a thing. On a tie, Tie shares the pot evenly.</string>
    <string name="rules_poker_people">Whose turn it is, when you may check, and who wins the hand. The app counts the chips; the game is yours.</string>
